  "text": "“This is for my grandmother,” he said. “My sister made it, but she’s too ill to come. I promised my sister I’d keep it safe.” He turned it a little, as if there might be an angle from which the wing still looked whole.\n\nI fetched some tape from the dock office. When I came back, he was holding the torn edges near each other. “Like this?” he asked. I bent down so I could see. “That looks right. Keep them there while I lay the tape.”\n\nEveryone had stepped off the last boat except a boy holding a paper bird. The operator waited while I went back to see whether he needed a hand. He stood very still, though the boat moved gently against the dock.\n\nTogether, we mended the wing. He held the paper steady while I laid the tape across the tear. We smoothed it gently. The folds his sister had made were still there. So was a small shiny line where we had worked.\n\nThe gathering began after the last boat returned and Mara had helped its passengers ashore. At the garden, Pip read The Torn Wing with her permission. Mara could sit and listen to the words she had written.\n\n“Mara still wants to come. Her work hours changed before the storm. She has to help the passengers off the last boat, and by then our gathering is over. She wrote this for you and asked me to bring it.”\n\nGrandma heard what had kept her friends away and shared The Empty Bench. The cushions were out again. Pip chose a real moment from the day for his own lantern, among the stories the garden already held.\n\nGrandma shared The Empty Bench, and Pip chose a real moment for his lantern. Afterward, he carried her story across the bridge to Mara. She took the copy at the dock to read when her work was finished.",
